 ### [Adaptive Octree Marching Tetrahedra](3897-adaptive-octree-marching-tetrahedra.md)
-An isosurface extraction method that uses an adaptive octree to subdivide a 3D volume only where the scalar field contains the target isovalue, applying the Marching Tetrahedra algorithm at leaf nodes to generate the mesh. It differs from its parent [Marching Tetrahedrons](3720-marching-tetrahedrons.md) by introducing spatial adaptivity via octree subdivision, reducing computation in empty regions, and from [Naive Isosurface Extraction](4328-naive-isosurface-extraction.md) by avoiding uniform grid traversal. Choose it when memory efficiency in sparse volumes is critical.
+An isosurface extraction method that uses an adaptive octree to subdivide a 3D volume only where the scalar field contains the target isovalue, applying the Marching Tetrahedra algorithm at leaf nodes to generate the mesh. It differs from its parent [Marching Tetrahedrons](3720-marching-tetrahedrons.md) by introducing spatial adaptivity via octree subdivision, reducing computation in empty regions, and from [Naive Isosurface Extraction](4328-naive-isosurface-extraction.md) by avoiding uniform grid traversal.
 
 ### [Naive Isosurface Extraction](4328-naive-isosurface-extraction.md)
-Naive Isosurface Extraction is a surface extraction algorithm that processes each tetrahedron in a tetrahedral mesh independently to generate a collection of individual, unconnected polygons. It solves the problem of identifying the intersection between a scalar field and a specific isovalue within a volumetric representation. Unlike optimized variants that ensure manifold connectivity through vertex reuse, this method creates new vertices for every intersection point, resulting in non-manifold surfaces with cracks at shared edges. It serves as the foundational, unoptimized baseline for understanding tetrahedral isosurface generation, building on the decomposition logic of [Marching Tetrahedrons](3720-marching-tetrahedrons.md). Choose it only for debugging or when connectivity is irrelevant.
+Naive Isosurface Extraction is a surface extraction algorithm that processes each tetrahedron independently to generate a collection of individual, unconnected polygons. It differs from standard Marching Tetrahedra by iterating through all pairs of sample points to find intersections with an isosurface, rather than using the standard 14-case lookup table based on vertex signs. This approach builds on [Marching Tetrahedrons](3720-marching-tetrahedrons.md) by operating on the tetrahedral subgrid structure.
+
+### [GPU Vertex Shader Isosurface Extraction](6706-gpu-vertex-shader-isosurface-extraction.md)
+GPU Vertex Shader Isosurface Extraction is a surface extraction technique that computes isosurfaces on programmable graphics hardware by drawing a single quadrilateral for each tetrahedron and computing intersection locations within the vertex shader. It sets itself apart from CPU-based or fragment-shader-based methods by leveraging the vertex shader's programmability to reposition vertices to exact intersection points, effectively moving the geometric computation from the CPU to the GPU's vertex processing stage.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of tetrahedra in the grid. Each tetrahedron is processed independently in $O(1)$ time [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of triangles generated. The output mesh size is typically larger than Marching Cubes for the same geometry due to the finer decomposition [S2, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Produces a mesh that matches voxel data better than Marching Cubes but creates significantly more vertices, making it slower and more memory-intensive [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Resolves topological ambiguities inherent in Marching Cubes (e.g., cases 6 and 7) by ensuring a consistent manifold surface through tetrahedral decomposition [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Higher vertex count leads to larger file sizes and increased rendering cost. Requires the input grid to be decomposable into tetrahedra (or an existing unstructured tetrahedral mesh) [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When topological correctness is critical and the ambiguity of Marching Cubes is unacceptable, or when working with unstructured tetrahedral grids [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** When performance and memory efficiency are paramount and the visual difference from Marching Cubes is negligible [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of tetrahedra (6 times the number of voxels). Each tetrahedron is processed independently in constant time.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$, where $M$ is the number of output triangles. The lookup table is constant size.
+*   **Performance:** The algorithm generates approximately 4x more geometry than [Marching Cubes](link-to-marching-cubes), making it slower in both computation and rendering [S4, unofficial][S5]. Benchmarks show MT producing significantly more vertices and faces than MC for the same input (e.g., 58,580 verts vs 15,268 verts in one demo) [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Produces a manifold surface without topological ambiguities (e.g., the "tetrahedron ambiguity" resolved by the decomposition) [S1, S4, unofficial]. It is simpler to implement than Marching Cubes due to fewer cases (16 total, reducible to 3 symmetric cases) [S4, unofficial][S5]. It works on unstructured grids as well as structured grids [S5].
+*   **Limitations:** Higher vertex count leads to increased memory usage and rendering cost [S4, unofficial]. The tetrahedral decomposition can introduce slight geometric bias depending on how the cube is split [S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When a manifold surface is required and topological correctness is more important than performance or mesh size [S4, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When performance is critical and the ambiguity of Marching Cubes can be handled or is acceptable [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Doi and Koide as an alternative to Marching Cubes [S4, unofficial]. It addresses the "tetrahedron ambiguity" where a single cube can be split in two topologically distinct ways [S1, unofficial]. By decomposing the cube into tetrahedra, the algorithm ensures a unique, consistent surface for each configuration [S1, unofficial]. It avoids the patent that covered Marching Cubes, which has since expired [S5]. The algorithm is significantly simpler than Marching Cubes because having only 4 points in a cell leads to merely 8 cases to consider, which can be reduced down to 3 with symmetry [S5]. It has enjoyed much research by GPGPU groups on accelerating it in hardware due to its simplicity and being essentially a triangle-driven algorithm [S5].
+Marching Tetrahedrons was introduced by Doi and Koide [S4, unofficial]. It was developed as an alternative to [Marching Cubes](link-to-marching-cubes) to address the topological ambiguities present in the cubic algorithm [S4, unofficial][S5]. A historical advantage was that it avoided the patent that covered Marching Cubes [S4, unofficial][S5]. The algorithm is essentially a triangle-driven approach, which has led to significant research by GPGPU groups on accelerating it in hardware [S5]. It is often implemented as a direct port of Paul Bourke's C implementation [S4, unofficial].
 
